\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The Morris–Lecar orbit at the larger current}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take the Morris–Lecar velocity of \ref{fnip-0005} at
#{I_{\mathrm{app}}=100}. \ref{fnip-0017} gives one equilibrium, the
unstable [focus](fnip-0035) of \ref{fnip-0037}, and an orbit that
returns to its starting point. The annulus of \ref{fnip-0055}
contains a [closed orbit](fnip-0052).}

\p{The upper drawing shows a computed orbit of this velocity. The
orbit started at #{(V_{*}+1.2,n_{*})} leaves the focus and approaches
this curve. The orbit started at #{(-60,0.05)} approaches it as well.
The open circle marks the focus. The middle drawing enlarges the
elliptical boundary and the first turn away from the focus. The lower
drawing is #{V} through two returns.}

\p{\citet{Figure 3.1b, p. 50}{ermentrout2010mathematical} is the
voltage at this current, and
\citet{§3.3.3, p. 55}{ermentrout2010mathematical} calls the closed
curve in the phase plane a limit cycle.
\citet{§4.1.3, pp. 96–97}{izhikevich2007dynamical} calls an isolated
periodic trajectory a limit cycle, and calls a stable one the phase
portrait of tonic spiking.}

